WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.080
It takes a lot of hard work and good luck
to get a movie made, but all of that can be

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.420
ruined in an instant with just one bad decision.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.820
Sometimes it doesn't matter how good the story
is or how famous the stars are, because a

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.270
single moment of stupidity can wash it all
away.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:20.580
Here's a look at some promising movies that
were cancelled because of a really dumb decision.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.480
At the Mountains of Madness

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.670
The masterwork of horror and science-fiction
author H.P.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.460
Lovecraft, At the Mountains of Madness has
long been a passion project for horror auteur

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:31.980
Guillermo del Toro.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.180
And del Toro seems like the perfect fit for
the tale of a group of scientists who discover

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.010
ancient, terrifyingly powerful entities in
Antarctica.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:45.899
Unfortunately, despite James Cameron producing
and interest from Tom Cruise, del Toro couldn't

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:50.650
get funding from Universal because he told
them it would be R-rated, meaning the teens

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.360
who usually flock to horror movies wouldn't
be able to see it.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.059
The project was cancelled, and del Toro later
said he "should have lied" to executives and

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:03.300
told them that At the Mountains of Madness
would be PG-13.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.179
Halo

00:01:05.179 --> 00:01:09.749
There were a lot of dumb decisions that led
to the demise of the proposed Halo movie,

00:01:09.749 --> 00:01:13.399
mostly because Microsoft apparently had no
idea how Hollywood works.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:18.060
They demanded a huge cut of profits and forced
studios to jump through hoops to even have

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.860
a chance to read the script.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.560
Plus, they wouldn't let Sony bid on the film
at all — because Sony's PlayStation is one

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.969
of the Microsoft Xbox's chief video game console
competitors.

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:33.630
Despite all that nonsense, Fox and Universal
were both still interested and teamed up.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.560
But then they made the final dumb decision
in this whole dumb process when Universal

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.829
hired up and comer Neill Blomkamp to direct.

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:44.619
Blomkamp was a fan of the game with a distinct
artistic vision.

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:47.450
The problem is, only Universal wanted him.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:52.219
Both Fox and Microsoft reportedly hated what
he was doing, wanting instead to make what

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:55.789
the director called a "generic, boring film
— something like G.I.

00:01:55.789 --> 00:01:57.689
Joe or some crap like that."

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:01.599
Ultimately, the only thing that came of it
was some test footage that teases the great

00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:03.630
Halo movie that could have been.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.630
And Blomkamp?

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.090
He went on to make District 9 instead.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:08.970
Great job, Microsoft!

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.959
Uncle Tom's Fairy Tales

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:17.410
In 1968, Richard Pryor wrote and starred in
an extremely dark, socially conscious movie

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.959
with the intentionally controversial title
Uncle Tom's Fairy Tales.

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:28.530
Pryor supposedly became so obsessed with making
the film, his wife reportedly "destroyed the

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.390
negative of the then still unfinished movie
in a fit a rage."

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.480
Nearly four decades later, another print of
the film emerged thanks to director Penelope

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.140
Spheeris, but so far it hasn't been released
either for legal reasons.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.560
Pyongyang

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.439
Based on Guy Delisle's intense graphic novel
about the struggles of living under the totalitarian

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:53.549
regime in North Korea, Pyongyang was supposed
to start shooting in early 2015 with Steve

00:02:53.549 --> 00:02:57.099
Carell in the lead role and Gore Verbinksi
as director.

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:00.700
Then Sony decided to release The Interview
in 2014.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.519
That reportedly led to the infamous hack of
the Sony servers in retaliation, not to mention

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:08.499
bomb threats against theaters that screened
The Interview.

00:03:08.499 --> 00:03:12.659
New Regency decided Pyongyang wasn't worth
the hassle and canceled the film entirely.

00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:16.120
The Beatles' version of The Lord of the Rings

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.120
J.R.R.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.409
Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings books rank
among the most beloved novels of all time.

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:26.959
First published as three separate volumes
in 1954, it took until 2001 for Peter Jackson's

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:30.079
epic, live-action adaptation to hit movie
theaters.

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:34.379
But there was an earlier version proposed
in the 1960s — and it would have starred

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:35.400
The Beatles.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.400
Yeah.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.959
Those Beatles.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:43.920
Besides doing a brand new double-album soundtrack,
the Beatles were set to star in the live action

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:49.780
adaptation, with Paul McCartney as Frodo,
Ringo Starr as Samwise, George Harrison as

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.239
Gandalf, and John Lennon as Gollum.

00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:58.379
Unfortunately, Tolkien wasn't a Beatles fan,
and he decided to nix the idea.

00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:03.030
Otherwise both music and cinematic history
would have been completely different… and

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.239
much, much weirder.

